Subject: unwritten extents gain a writer

The machinery for unwritten extents was almost all here, and almost all
unreachable. Reads treated them as holes; ext4_extent_get_blocks converted
them in place on write, zeroing what the write does not cover and splitting
in all three geometries; truncate masked the state bit out of every length.
What did not exist was a way to create one -- so none of it ever ran, and
preallocation (fallocate's whole point: blocks without content, zeros
without writing them) was unimplementable.

ext4_extent_preallocate() is the missing origin: back a logical range with
extents marked unwritten, skipping ranges already backed by either state.
ext4_extent_probe() is its witness -- it reports what backs a block and
whether the extent is unwritten, a question the mapping API cannot answer
because it folds unwritten and hole into the same zero.

Three latent defects had to be fixed for the dormant machinery to be safe
to wake, and each was the kind that corrupts silently:

  * the merge predicates tested only adjacency, so a written extent could
    merge into an unwritten neighbour and inherit its state -- data
    reinterpreted as zeros, or preallocated garbage exposed as data. They
    now require equal state, as Linux's do.
  * ext4_ext_zero_unwritten_range left its zeroed blocks dirty in the block
    cache while file data is written directly to the device; a later cache
    flush would have replaced the data with the zeros. It zeroes on the
    medium and invalidates any cached copy instead -- and stops routing
    file-data blocks into the journal on the way.
  * ext4_fs_truncate_inode decided by size alone, so truncate(0) of a
    zero-length preallocated file was a no-op -- and every unlink truncates
    to 0 first, so preallocated blocks leaked on delete. The sweep now runs
    whenever blocks exist past the target, not only when the size shrinks.

Allocation is block-at-a-time, coalesced by the (fixed) merge; a run
allocator in ext4_balloc is the known follow-up -- 256 MB preallocates in
about a second, which is usable and not proud.

Verified by Tests/run_prealloc_tests.sh: unwritten extents confirmed by
debugfs's own [u] markers, reads return zeros over deliberately marker-
filled reused blocks, first-write conversion keeps the data and zeros the
rest, truncate and unlink leak nothing, e2fsck clean throughout, and every
crash cut of the whole lifecycle recovers through Linux-kernel replay.
